"""P1: do extra reads substitute for layers? Generated by build.py - do not edit.

THE CLAIM UNDER TEST. Hop budget multiplies across layers: ours is n_layers x n_read, theirs is
n_layers x 1. The controlled comparison is NL2 r4 against NL2 r1 - identical parameters and
layers, differing only in n_read - across chain depth, against depth-matched (NL2) and deep (NL8)
transformer baselines.

Read accuracy against FLOOR, the better of two query-free heuristics printed per depth: guessing
among sinks, and the dmode=edge shortcut (the sink whose source is also a target), which scores
1.000 at every depth on the v1-v4 task. Per-seed accuracies are printed; read gaps against them.

Sources concatenated verbatim from experiments/ by build.py. Nothing is retyped.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import os
import subprocess
import sys

#   THE CHEAP PROBE SETTINGS, BAKED IN. Kaggle metadata cannot pass environment variables, so a
#   bundle without this would run gpu_run's DEFAULTS - STEPS=6000, D=256, DEPTHS=2,3,4,6, every
#   arm - which is the 5.5-hour full sweep, not the ~15-minute probe. Exactly the expensive run
#   we are trying to avoid committing to before P1 is answered.
#   setdefault, not assignment, so anything set outside still wins.
#   THE 30-MINUTE DESIGN, chosen after the eps ablation showed chain accuracy swinging 0.074 to
#   0.496 across seeds at IDENTICAL settings. A single-seed ordering between three arms would be
#   a coin flip, and P1 is an ordering claim - so the budget goes on SEEDS, not on breadth.
#
#   DEPTHS 2 AND 3, after v2 at depth 6 returned a pure null - every arm BELOW the sink floor,
#   transformer ceiling included at 0.049 against a floor of 0.143. Depth 6 is the most
#   DISCRIMINATIVE cell (NL2 r1 has budget 2 and provably cannot reach) but it is not LEARNABLE
#   at this scale, and ceiling_sweep had already said so: at 6000 steps even mqar-3 - one hop,
#   three pairs - reached only 0.292.
#
#   Depth 3 keeps the discrimination and drops most of the difficulty: with NL=2 it still exceeds
#   the layer count, so NL2 r1 remains budget-limited while NL2 r4 does not. Depth 2 rides along
#   as the control every arm should manage, and the ceiling then REPORTS which cells are readable
#   instead of my guessing a second time.
#
#   v4 DROPS NL8 r1 AND ADDS DEPTH 4. v3 showed NL8 r1 at 0.374 on depth 3 - WORSE than NL2 r1's
#   0.586 despite hop budget 8 against 2 - which the budget model cannot produce. An 8-layer model
#   simply does not train in 3000 steps, so that arm measured trainability, not capacity, and any
#   parameter-efficiency claim resting on it would be a win over a baseline weak for the wrong
#   reason. Buying it a fair fight needs 8000+ steps; that is a later run, not this one.
#
#   What v3 DID establish is clean, because NL2 r1 and NL2 r4 have IDENTICAL parameters (341,947)
#   and identical layers, differing only in n_read: 0.997 -> 1.000 at depth 2 (reads unnecessary)
#   and 0.586 -> 0.879 at depth 3 (reads necessary). Depth 4 asks whether that gap WIDENS, which a
#   single point cannot show and a trend across three depths can. Sink floor there is 0.200, and
#   depth 4 at ratio 1.0 needs 5 + 8 = 13 entities against NENT=32, so batch() cannot hang.
#
#   NENT=32 is not arbitrary. depth=6 at ratio=1.0 needs (depth+1) + 2*round(depth*ratio) = 19
#   distinct entities; at NENT=16 make_chain returns None every time and batch() loops forever
#   waiting for a sequence that cannot be built.
#
#   v5 (audit 2026-09-15). ARMS was still 'ceiling_NL8,...' after 08fe8b3 added the depth-matched
#   NL2 ceiling, so a plain rebuild would have picked up CEIL_MULT and silently never run the new
#   arm. 'ceiling' now selects BOTH. The task default is dmode=chain (gpu_run), because the v1-v4
#   task has a query-free shortcut scoring 1.000 at every depth; set DMODE=edge to reproduce them.
os.environ.setdefault('MODE', 'ceiling_triage')
os.environ.setdefault('STEPS', '30000')
os.environ.setdefault('CTRL_STEPS', '2000')
os.environ.setdefault('SEEDS', '0')
for _k, _v in (('STEPS', '3000'), ('SEEDS', '0,1,2'), ('D', '128'), ('HEAD_DIM', '64'),
               ('NENT', '32'), ('DEPTHS', '2,3,4'), ('DMODE', 'chain'), ('RATIO', '1.0'),
               ('ARMS', 'ceiling,tied_NL2_r4,tied_NL2_r1')):
    os.environ.setdefault(_k, _v)
print('BUNDLE ENV  ' + '  '.join('%s=%s' % (k, os.environ.get(k, '-')) for k in (
    'MODE', 'STEPS', 'SEEDS', 'D', 'HEAD_DIM', 'NENT', 'DEPTHS', 'DMODE', 'RATIO', 'ARMS')), flush=True)

#   PINNED. 0.5.2 is the newest release before v4 ran (2026-09-14) and so the one whose kernel
#   matched our loop; an unpinned install could pick up a layout or signature change mid-series.
print('installing flash-linear-attention==0.5.2 ...', flush=True)
subprocess.run([sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', 'install', '-q', 'flash-linear-attention==0.5.2'],
               check=False)


